        "id": "ary-server",
        "intent": "Serve a read-only API (refs, commit log) and the existing HTML view over loopback",
        "necessity": "required",
        "serves": "task",
        "removal_impact": "There would be no server: nothing to list branches or commits and nothing to render a page on demand.",
        "evidence": "Ran the command, curled /api/refs, /api/log and /view, and loaded the shell in headless Chromium; test/ary.test.ts runs the real command.",
        "hunks": [
          "h5:1-72,74-77,91-108",
          "h8:1-9,48-57,61-81,88-104",
          "h12"
        ],
        "notes": "Browsing writes nothing: `wip` is read from the snapshot ref, never taken. Git runs synchronously per request. The --port argument is validated as digits only (Number('') would quietly mean any port). Known quirk: `wip` is special-cased, so a branch named wip can't be opened by that name (use heads/wip; the README says so). Commit search runs git log twice (--grep and --author, which git ANDs together), merges the shas, and pages by hand: this git ignores --no-walk when -n is given, so -n/--skip can't be used for it."
      },
      {
        "id": "ary-host-check",
        "intent": "Answer only requests addressed to this server's own host names, and say which",
        "necessity": "required",
        "serves": "task",
        "removal_impact": "Any web page open in your browser could make requests to the local server, and with DNS rebinding read the answers: your branches, commit messages and the code of every commit.",
        "evidence": "test/ary.test.ts sends a request with a foreign Host header, expects 403, and checks that the message names the allowed addresses.",
        "hunks": [
          "h8:82-86"
        ],
        "notes": "Graded required rather than defensive: a server on your machine that serves your source is unsafe without it. It is a few lines, so it is split out to be seen on its own. Only 127.0.0.1:<port> and localhost:<port> are allowed (the server doesn't listen on IPv6, so [::1] would be dead code). The refusal names the allowed addresses; the port is part of the check, so an SSH tunnel must use the same port number. It is required by a safety bar (a local server that serves your source must not answer other sites), not by anything the task asked for. Host names are matched case-insensitively."
      },
      {
        "id": "ary-guards",
        "intent": "Validate requests, cap their size, refuse non-GET, and set safe headers",
        "necessity": "defensive",
        "serves": "ary-server",
        "removal_impact": "Malformed or hostile parameters would reach git instead of a clean 400 (the single-commit path is already safe from flags, see git-end-of-options, so this is a second layer there and mainly gives a clear error); a 200+ commit range would hang the browser; a huge limit or a bad number would be passed through (limit=0 once gave an empty page that claimed there was more); a POST would get an answer; pages would be cached and sniffed.",
        "evidence": "test/ary.test.ts checks the 400, 405 and 422 responses and the cap.",
        "hunks": [
          "h8:10-47,58-60,87"
        ],
        "notes": "The 200-commit cap is a product judgment: the API would work without it. Ranges are also protected by --end-of-options in git.ts; the leading-'-' check is a second layer, and the single-commit path in git.ts also has --end-of-options (see git-end-of-options), so the leading-'-' check is a second layer there as well. This group mixes kinds of change: validation and the limit clamps (one of which fixed a real bug), the cap (a product judgment), and headers and the 405 (defense in depth). This group also holds the CSP and no-store/nosniff headers and the limit/skip clamps. The 200-character cap on a search term is also a guard, but it sits on the /api/log route line, which is in ary-server's range. Search text goes to git as --grep=<text> with fixed-string matching, so it can't be read as an option or a pattern. The Host check, which protects something real, is in ary-host-check. Why the CSP and nosniff headers are graded defensive, not required: the pages already put all repo text through textContent or escaping, and the view page carries its own nonce CSP, so these headers are a second layer behind that. A review argued they are as essential as the Host check; the difference is that without the Host check nothing else stops a foreign page reading the server, while without these headers the escaping still holds. Numbers in limit/skip that are out of range are ignored in favour of the defaults; limit=0 used to produce an empty page that claimed there was more."
      },
