    "risks": [
      "The shell's JavaScript has no automated tests; only the server is tested, and the shell only for parsing.",
      "The view page is framed unsandboxed and is same-origin with the API: if hostile commit text ever got past its escaping and nonce CSP, a script could read the repo through the read-only API.",
      "Git commands run synchronously, so a slow /view stalls the sidebar too (single-user local tool).",
      "A branch named wip can't be opened by name in the revision box; heads/wip works.",
      "Every /api/log call reads the notes lists for two refs, and a broad search runs two full git log passes, so large repos will feel it (and 'Load more' repeats it).",
      "Remote HEAD pointers are hidden by name (endsWith('/HEAD')), so a branch literally named .../HEAD would also be hidden.",
      "Search results are ordered by commit date, which can differ from the unfiltered log order on histories with skewed dates; the tests only use linear histories.",
      "A random port means browser bookmarks don't survive a restart unless --port is given.",
      "The Host check includes the port, so reaching the server through an SSH tunnel on a different local port is refused (the message says to use the same port).",
      "An error from git (a bad range, a malformed base) is returned as text, which can include local paths. It is shown as text and the server is local only.",
      "resolveCommit now needs a git whose rev-parse accepts --end-of-options; older gits would fail every command that resolves a revision.",
      "Another site's page can still fire simple GET requests at the server if it guesses the port (the Host check stops reads, not requests); each triggers synchronous git work. Low impact; a --port with a well-known value makes guessing easier.",
      "A broad search on a big repo runs two full git log passes plus a --stdin pass with every matching sha, on the one thread, so it can freeze /view and the sidebar until it finishes."
    ]
